{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 34, :comments ["<b>And the dead animal shall be his</b> — to the injured party. And they assess the carcass and he takes it against the payment, and the one who caused the damage pays him the compensation for his damage. This is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Exodus 21:34). But he did not clarify its ruling, for there is no need to say regarding this carcass that the injured party takes it as part of the reckoning of the payments when he brings it before the court to pay the damage, since even if the one who caused the damage had other carcasses and animals that had been rendered טְרֵפוֹת in his house, he could discharge his obligation with them according to their value — for we have already said that this includes anything worth money, even bran (Bava Kamma 7a). Rather, the point is to say that the carcass belongs to the injured party and is considered his property; therefore, if its value declined afterward, or if it was stolen, the one who caused the damage is liable to pay only the value at the time of death. If the ox was worth a maneh when alive and worth fifty zuz when dead, he owes fifty zuz, and this is the injured party's concern — he must attend to the carcass and keep it for himself. And this ruling applies to all cases of damage. This is what the Sages call פְּחַת נְבֵלָה (diminution of the carcass), and it is explicit in the Gemara (ibid. 10a)."], :book :exodus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :notes {0 "Ramban cites Bava Kamma 7a and then refers to 'ibid. 10a' (שם י:), both tractate Bava Kamma."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:13:49.595394385Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}